CASE STUDY 01

The Agentic Gap
Everyone can join and transcribe. No one communicates behavior , that is the gap.
 CapabilitiesZoom AIMicrosoft CopilotProject Luna
Join Meetings
✓✓✓
Transcribe
✓✓✓
Answer Questions & Take Action
✓✓✓
Communicate Behavioral Presence
✕✕✓
▲  THE CORE PROBLEM

Designing Non-Verbal Communication
Humans constantly signal intent before speaking.
Eye contact.
Breathing.
Pauses.
Movement.
AI should do the same.
I designed behavioral cues that reduce uncertainty.
Signaled intent lowers cognitive load. Luna restores the missing 
nonverbal cues through motion, color, and animation.

Luna’s presence had to be visible to everyone, not because she needed a tile, but because the tile removed a 
layer of user uncertainty.
BEFORE
AI beside the conversation
Zoom AI Companion and Microsoft Copilot can assist during a 
live meeting, but the AI experience remains visually separate 
from the people in the conversation.
The user has to look somewhere else to understand what the AI 
is doing, what it has captured, or how it is responding. 

The AI could participate in the meeting, but it didn't have a 
visible seat in it.
DECISION
Luna inside the meeting
Her presence and state became part of the shared meeting 
context. Users could see where she was, what state she was 
in, and when she was participating without leaving the call.
WHAT IT SOLVED
Fewer steps. Less context switching. Less ambiguity. Luna’s behavioral presence became legible in the same 
place the work was happening.
Product principle: AI presence should reduce cognitive load, not create another place to look.
Giving Luna a Seat in the Call

h
From Meeting Tool to Teammate
Today's meeting AI assist the meeting. Luna participates in it.
AI Assists
AI sits along the conversation. Summarizing answering and capturing.
AI Participates
Luna is part of the conversation, listening, understanding the context 
and contributing in the moment
The shift wasn't just where AI appeared. It was what AI became.
Luna didn't belong beside the meeting — she belonged inside of it.

Giving Luna a Seat in the Call
1
Guest participant
Luna appears as her own tile in the call, 
right alongside the people.
2
Joins like a guest
She enters the meeting the way any guest 
does through the browser, no extra 
hardware.
3
Always in view
Her state stays visible in the call, so the 
team never wonders what she's doing.
A seat in the call changes everything.
Luna became a real participant: visible, present, in the room.
Presence had to be literal. For Luna to feel like a teammate, she couldn't live in a side window , she had to appear in the meeting 
itself, as a participant everyone can see.

Directing the System
→
→
Natural language became a design medium.
I directed AI to rapidly prototype, validate, and refine interaction behavior.

Idea → build one → test → observe → refine → repeat. Hundreds of micro-decisions — every one was a design call.

