Making Invisible States Visible
Every AI has invisible states. Most AI hides them. Luna exposes them , that’s the innovation.
Listening   “I’m being heard”
soft purple 
calm, ambient, trustworthy; slow motion so 
attention never feels like surveillance
Reasoning   “it’s thinking”
brighter purple
shimmering particles , thinking made visible, 
replacing dead air
Speaking   “it’s talking”
green 
 the ‘live’ cue we already trust from mic and 
record lights
Trust grows when internal state becomes externally visible.

Luna, Live
Live capture — listening, reasoning, and speaking states in motion during a real call

Results & Impact
What happened after I presented this work.  
01
Validated the gap
My team  agreed the missing behavioral 
states were a real oversight worth 
solving.
02
Shaped the product
The team chose to move Luna directly 
into Google Meet instead of keeping her 
in a standalone hub.
03
Expanded the mandate
The initiative extended my scope to 
additional  projects, including 3D 
rendering explorations.
Impact doesn't have to be a launch , a validated oversight, a product decision, or new scope all show the difference this work made.

Design Principles I Learned
●Designing AI means designing behavior.

●Behavior is part of the product architecture.

●Trust depends on legibility.

●Interaction precedes intelligence.

●The strongest AI experiences communicate intent before action.

Luna explores how behavioral design can transform an intelligent system into a trusted collaborator.

CASE STUDY 02
Bank of Daniel
Turning completed transactions into 
reusable shopping lists.
ROLE  
UX Designer & Researcher
LENGTH  
1 Week
TYPE  
End-to-end Product
Digital Receipt

Your bank shows the money , not what you bought.
Turn a completed transaction into a useful, understandable record of what was actually bought.
TODAY — A BLACK BOX
Tap a transaction and you see a dollar amount, date, type, 
and card number. No context , you don't know what you 
actually bought.
→
PRODUCT DIRECTION  —  A GLASS BOX
One place to see the amount and the itemized receipt , 
aggregated across every vendor. No app-hopping.
j
One view. One purchase history. One place to act.
See
only the amount, date, type, and card number.
Search
across email, retailer apps, and paper receipts to 
reconstruct a purchase.
Memory
what was bought without the context needed to 
make the transaction useful.
See
the items behind the transaction, not just the 
dollar amount.
Find
the receipt and purchase context in one place.
Reuse
completed purchases as a shopping list for the 
next trip.

From Payment to Purchase Context
Connect the receipt to the transaction so I can see what I bought and turn individual items into a shopping list.
THE PROBLEM
The transaction isn't enough
A transaction tells me where, when, and how 
much I paid  but not the items I purchased.
The receipt is disconnected
The details I need may live in email, a retailer 
app, or a paper receipt instead of with the 
payment.
The purchase is hard to use
Even when I find the receipt, I still have to 
manually turn individual items into a shopping 
list.
Because payment completes the transaction , not the 
user's task.
After I pay, I may still want to understand what I bought and organize 
the items I care about. The experience can continue from the 
payment into the purchase.
The transaction is already the natural anchor.
I'm already in my banking app looking at the purchase. Attaching the 
receipt keeps payment and purchase context together and gives me 
a path to act on the items.
TRANSACTION——RECEIPT——ITEM——SHOPPING LIST

The transaction is only the starting point.
It doesn't tell me what I bought.
The purchase is disconnected
The transaction contains payment information, but not the 
item-level details behind the purchase.
The receipt lives somewhere else
Customers leave the banking app to find receipts, initiate returns, 
or check what they purchased.
The information has to be reconstructed
When customers want to buy something again or resolve a 
purchase, they find the same information across multiple places.
TODAY, TAPPING A TRANSACTION 
SHOWS ONLY:
Transaction Date
↓
Transaction Type
↓
Card Number

Find the purchase behind the transaction.
Search by merchant or item, or open a transaction directly. The purchase details 
are connected to the transaction.
Search by what you remember
Search by merchant or item instead of remembering when the transaction 
occurred.
Open any transaction
Tap a transaction as you always have  date, type, and card number are still 
there.
The receipt is attached
Scroll down on the transaction to see the itemized receipt, pulled in from the 
merchant.

Keep shopping lists connected to your purchases.
Saved items live in one place and remain connected to the transactions 
they came from.
Manage lists
Open “My Lists” alongside your accounts and manage saved items in 
one place.
Add context
Add a size, color, or personal note to an item.
Keep the purchase connected
Every saved item links back to the original transaction for purchase 
details.
Remove an item
Remove items when they are no longer relevant.

Build lists for what you'll buy next.
Create lists from items you've already purchased and organize them for 
future shopping.
Create a list
Start with any saved item and organize it around a need, store, or 
occasion.
Make it recurring
Reuse a list for routines like weekly grocery shopping.
Organize by purpose
Keep lists separate by store, project, or occasion.

A transaction becomes a starting point.
Less searching
Purchase information stays 
connected to the transaction.
More useful transactions
A completed purchase can 
become the source for a future 
shopping list.
More value from existing data
Merchant-provided purchase 
details can make transaction history 
more useful to customers.
Bank of Daniel already tells me where my money went.
This experience helps me remember what I bought — and turn it into a shopping list for later.

